WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.246 --> 00:00:01.672
- There's a loop, I'm out of it.

00:00:01.672 --> 00:00:03.235
I don't know anything about children.

00:00:13.043 --> 00:00:14.749
- I currently don't have
any plans to have children

00:00:14.749 --> 00:00:17.788
any time remotely soon, I'm
thinking maybe when I'm 50.

00:00:17.788 --> 00:00:20.659
- My wife and I definitely want
kids, maybe some time soon.

00:00:20.659 --> 00:00:22.048
- 2 1/2, I want 2 1/2.

00:00:22.048 --> 00:00:23.019
- So you can have half of one of mine.

00:00:23.019 --> 00:00:24.708
- One slightly smaller child.

00:00:24.708 --> 00:00:27.194
- I'm just trying to take
care of my plants right now.

00:00:27.194 --> 00:00:28.666
I'm not doing a very good job.

00:00:36.602 --> 00:00:38.420
- What is the estimated annual cost

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:41.548
of having a single, five-year-old child?

00:00:41.548 --> 00:00:43.344
- $16,210.

00:00:43.344 --> 00:00:45.697
- $22,374.

00:00:45.697 --> 00:00:48.453
- $30,106.

00:00:48.453 --> 00:00:49.824
- Ooh.

00:00:49.824 --> 00:00:51.132
- You gotta get their hair cut.

00:00:51.132 --> 00:00:52.651
- Also shots.

00:00:54.388 --> 00:00:55.943
- Five-year-olds shouldn't be drinking.

00:00:55.943 --> 00:00:57.516
- I'm going A on this.

00:00:57.516 --> 00:00:59.268
- C, a lot of money.

00:00:59.268 --> 00:01:00.062
(bell chimes)
^ - [Voiceover] A.

00:01:00.062 --> 00:01:01.113
- Yes.

00:01:01.113 --> 00:01:02.476
- $16,000?

00:01:02.476 --> 00:01:04.781
I can't afford a 28-year-old man.

00:01:04.781 --> 00:01:06.107
- If they're this cheap,

00:01:07.139 --> 00:01:08.699
10 minutes from now I might be like,

00:01:08.699 --> 00:01:10.965
"Cool, I'm gonna get out
there and have five kids."

00:01:10.965 --> 00:01:12.784
- According to the USDA,

00:01:12.784 --> 00:01:14.816
United States Department of Agriculture,

00:01:14.816 --> 00:01:16.999
the average percent of
household expenditures

00:01:16.999 --> 00:01:19.343
attributable to one child is?

00:01:19.343 --> 00:01:20.280
- 15%.

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:21.030
- 22%.

00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:22.446
- 27%.

00:01:22.446 --> 00:01:23.834
- Then you divide it by 15.

00:01:23.834 --> 00:01:25.303
15, 30.

00:01:25.303 --> 00:01:27.325
- My gut has been wrong both times so far,

00:01:27.325 --> 00:01:28.462
but I'm gonna go with it again.

00:01:29.684 --> 00:01:30.867
- It's roughly 20%.

00:01:32.051 --> 00:01:33.343
- 22%.

00:01:33.343 --> 00:01:34.526
(bell chimes)
^ - [Voiceover] C, 27%.

00:01:35.633 --> 00:01:36.338
- This is rigged.

00:01:36.338 --> 00:01:37.480
- I mean, I don't know.

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:38.832
These are some frickin' hard questions.

00:01:39.831 --> 00:01:41.098
- What does the rest of that money go to?

00:01:41.098 --> 00:01:42.568
- It's like car payments.

00:01:42.568 --> 00:01:44.433
- Car payments, fish tanks.

00:01:45.497 --> 00:01:47.211
Like adult fish tanks.

00:01:47.211 --> 00:01:49.302
- What is the largest total expenditure

00:01:49.302 --> 00:01:52.564
on a child in a two-children family?

00:01:52.564 --> 00:01:53.221
- Health care.

00:01:53.221 --> 00:01:54.785
- Child care/education.

00:01:54.785 --> 00:01:55.815
- Housing.

00:01:57.868 --> 00:01:59.677
- Any time I see a kid, I
just don't approach them

00:01:59.677 --> 00:02:01.860
because they're usually
sneezing or coughing.

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:02.800
They do that kid cough.

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:04.002
- Kids are gross.

00:02:04.002 --> 00:02:06.171
- B has never done me wrong,
except at the beginning.

00:02:06.171 --> 00:02:07.141
So I'm sticking with B.

00:02:07.141 --> 00:02:07.935
(bell chimes)
^ - [Voiceover] C, housing.

00:02:07.935 --> 00:02:09.930
- Yes, woo, woo.

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:11.624
- What kind of houses
are people living in?

00:02:11.624 --> 00:02:13.795
- Can't you just stack
the kids like Legos?

00:02:13.795 --> 00:02:15.359
- I'm taking away your kids.

00:02:15.359 --> 00:02:18.501
- What is the estimated
total cost of raising a child

00:02:18.501 --> 00:02:23.501
up to 18, including all of
17, without college expenses?

00:02:23.521 --> 00:02:25.128
- $245,000.

00:02:25.128 --> 00:02:28.075
- $316,534.

00:02:28.075 --> 00:02:31.633
- $372,812.

00:02:31.633 --> 00:02:32.779
- Well, first of all, I just wanna say

00:02:32.779 --> 00:02:34.920
all three of these numbers make me ill.

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:37.631
- 112 plus.

00:02:37.631 --> 00:02:38.544
- Quarter of a million dollars.

00:02:38.544 --> 00:02:39.989
It costs a quarter of a million dollars

00:02:39.989 --> 00:02:41.767
to raise your child to the age of 18.

00:02:41.767 --> 00:02:43.230
- The one goes up there.

00:02:44.735 --> 00:02:45.900
Hold on, hold on, hold on.

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:47.777
- Okay.

00:02:47.777 --> 00:02:49.425
- $245,000.

00:02:49.425 --> 00:02:50.953
- [Voiceover] It's A,
(bell chimes) $245,000.

00:02:50.953 --> 00:02:53.737
- Oh, yes!

00:02:53.737 --> 00:02:56.757
- You can get some really
nice shoes for $245,000.

00:02:56.757 --> 00:02:57.785
- Yeah, you could.

00:02:57.785 --> 00:02:58.893
- Maybe two pairs.

00:02:58.893 --> 00:03:00.503
- So either go on a lot of vacations

00:03:00.503 --> 00:03:03.548
or you don't go on vacation,
stick around the home,

00:03:03.548 --> 00:03:04.991
have a bunch of little
yous running around,

00:03:04.991 --> 00:03:07.026
they'll grow up, tell the world about you.

00:03:07.026 --> 00:03:08.670
- I think I'm still
leaning towards vacation.

00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:09.956
- Okay.

00:03:09.956 --> 00:03:12.370
- [Man] I'm surprised,
it is really expensive.

00:03:12.370 --> 00:03:15.166
- [Man 2] It's a decision
that you can't take lightly,

00:03:15.166 --> 00:03:17.086
no matter what stage of
the process you're in.

00:03:17.086 --> 00:03:18.314
- [Together] Thanks, Mom.

00:03:18.314 --> 00:03:19.786
- You coulda had a Ferrari.

